(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-cms-exam-5-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 5 Master Exam — Form A","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"An 84-year-old woman spent 9 days in the hospital for a large pericardial effusion that needed two procedures. She is now weak and needs help to walk. Besides cardiology and cardiothoracic surgery, which service is most appropriate before she goes home?","choices":["Rehabilitation","Infectious disease","Genetics","Nephrology"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Older adults are more likely to need rehabilitation after prolonged illness and procedures, which also carry higher morbidity and mortality at her age. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which surgery treats aortic dissection?","choices":["Ultrasound-guided thrombin injection","Grafting and replacing the aorta","Catheter-directed thrombolysis","Carotid endarterectomy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Surgery for dissection grafts and replaces the diseased portion of the aorta, after blood pressure is lowered with esmolol or nitroprusside and pain is managed. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man on telemetry has a prolonged QT after starting a new medication and then develops polymorphic ventricular tachycardia (QRS complexes that vary in shape and twist around the baseline). He becomes unresponsive and pulseless. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous magnesium first","Transcutaneous pacing","Defibrillation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pulseless polymorphic ventricular tachycardia, including torsades, is treated as cardiac arrest with defibrillation and cardiopulmonary resuscitation; magnesium follows. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with long-standing diabetes describes a continuous burning sensation in both feet that is the same at rest and during exercise. Her pedal pulses are easily palpable. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Peripheral neuropathy","Critical limb ischemia","Spinal stenosis","Peripheral artery disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Peripheral neuropathy, common in older patients with diabetes or alcoholism, causes a continuous burning sensation in the foot that is unaffected by exercise, unlike arterial claudication. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man has a 12-second run of identical wide QRS complexes at 160 per minute on telemetry. He stays alert, with a normal blood pressure, and feels only a flutter. How should this run be classified?","choices":["Non-sustained, polymorphic","Sustained, monomorphic","Non-sustained, monomorphic","Sustained, polymorphic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Under 30 seconds without hemodynamic collapse is non-sustained, and identical complexes make it monomorphic ventricular tachycardia. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with dilated cardiomyopathy has orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat), crackles at both lung bases and diminished breath sounds at the right base. A chest radiograph is ordered. Which finding on the film would indicate pulmonary edema?","choices":["A normal film that excludes congestion","Short horizontal lines meeting the pleura","A small heart with clear lung fields","Isolated thickening of the pericardium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Kerley B lines are horizontal lines 1 to 2 cm long that meet the pleura at right angles and indicate pulmonary edema. Cardiomegaly and pleural effusions are the other expected radiograph findings. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which electrocardiogram changes occur in Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Left bundle branch block only","Low QRS voltage in all leads","ST elevation and T wave inversion","Inferior and lateral Q waves"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The electrocardiogram shows ST segment elevation and T wave inversion, which is why the syndrome is managed like acute coronary syndrome until catheterization. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman has sudden chest pain and diaphoresis an hour after learning she has lost her savings in a financial collapse. The electrocardiogram shows ST elevation and troponin is elevated. No catheterization has been done yet. Which is the most appropriate initial approach?","choices":["Discharge home with grief counseling","Follow acute coronary syndrome protocols","Start a positive inotrope infusion","Alcohol ablation of the septum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stress cardiomyopathy mimics acute coronary syndrome, and because there are no reliable early differences, evaluation and treatment follow the established protocols for acute coronary syndrome patients. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with stable cardiomyopathy on full medical therapy says he tires after walking a block and feels anxious about exercising. He has no symptoms at rest. Which referral is most appropriate to improve his exercise tolerance?","choices":["Hospice referral for end-stage care","A competitive sports clearance clinic","Genetic screening of his children","A cardiac rehabilitation program"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac rehabilitation programs improve exercise tolerance and quality of life, with education on medication adherence, nutrition and symptom monitoring, and psychological support for chronic illness. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"Which is the first treatment for torsades de pointes in a conscious patient?","choices":["Intravenous procainamide","Intravenous amiodarone","Intravenous metoprolol","Intravenous magnesium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In a conscious patient, intravenous magnesium breaks torsades, followed by more magnesium as an infusion. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What is the most frequent presumed cause of myocarditis in developed countries?","choices":["Viral infection","Autoimmune disease","Drug reactions","Bacterial infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In developed countries viral infection is presumed to be the most frequent cause, including parvovirus B19, enteroviruses such as Coxsackie group B, adenoviruses and hepatitis C. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with recurrent urinary tract infections has aortic valve endocarditis, and his blood cultures grow Enterococcus faecalis. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Nafcillin plus rifampin","Cefazolin alone","Oral amoxicillin once","Ampicillin plus gentamicin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Enterococcal endocarditis is treated with ampicillin (or penicillin G) plus gentamicin, ampicillin plus ceftriaxone, or vancomycin plus gentamicin. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Erythema extending well beyond the margin of an inflamed vein raises suspicion of which condition?","choices":["Septic thrombophlebitis","Superficial phlebitis","Lipodermatosclerosis","Stasis dermatitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. When erythema extends significantly beyond the margin of the vein, septic thrombophlebitis should be suspected; simple thrombophlebitis keeps its redness along the vein's course. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"What must follow chest or back pain in a patient with a known thoracic aortic aneurysm?","choices":["Urgent imaging","Routine yearly imaging","Reassurance and analgesia","An exercise stress test"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Chest or back pain with a known or suspected thoracic aortic aneurysm must undergo urgent imaging to rule out the aneurysm as the cause of the pain. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 108"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man has 3 weeks of fever and night sweats. Two separate blood cultures grow Streptococcus gallolyticus, and a transesophageal echocardiogram shows an oscillating mass on the aortic valve. He has no skin, eye or kidney findings. How is his case classified under the modified Duke criteria?","choices":["Possible endocarditis","Unclassifiable for now","Endocarditis ruled out","Definite endocarditis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Two major criteria are met: a typical organism (Streptococcus gallolyticus) in two separate cultures and an oscillating mass on echocardiogram. Two major criteria alone establish definite endocarditis. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 19-year-old swimmer has an electrocardiogram before a sports physical. She has no symptoms. The rate is 72, with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS, but the beats come closer together as she breathes in and spread apart as she breathes out. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Sinus arrhythmia","Sick sinus syndrome","Atrial fibrillation","Multifocal atrial tachycardia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A normal P wave before every narrow QRS at 60 to 100, with the rate rising on inspiration and falling on expiration, is sinus arrhythmia: a normal, physiologic rhythm that is irregular with a pattern. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has superficial thrombophlebitis of the forearm after an intravenous catheter and asks whether the clot can travel to her lungs. Which statement about her outlook is accurate?","choices":["Pulmonary embolism is the usual outcome","Serious complications are rare","Chronic venous insufficiency follows in most","Fatal embolism is likely without treatment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Superficial phlebitis and thrombophlebitis rarely cause serious complications and rarely embolize, which distinguishes them from deep vein thrombosis, where pulmonary embolism is the major complication. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"A 32-year-old man with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and outflow tract obstruction has breathlessness and chest tightness on exertion. His blood pressure is 124/78 and heart rate is 76. He takes no cardiac medication yet. Which is the initial medication for his outflow obstruction?","choices":["A high-intensity loop diuretic","A beta blocker such as metoprolol","A nitrate such as nitroglycerin","A positive inotrope such as digoxin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A beta blocker such as metoprolol is the initial medication for left ventricular outflow tract obstruction; it slows the heart and improves diastolic filling. Verapamil or diltiazem is the alternative. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man has a regular tachycardia at 170 with an identical, abnormally shaped P wave before every QRS. Adenosine briefly slows the ventricular response, but the abnormal atrial rhythm continues unchanged afterward. Which rhythm does this response point to?","choices":["Atrioventricular nodal reentry","Orthodromic reentrant tachycardia","Focal atrial tachycardia","Multifocal atrial tachycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Adenosine is usually less effective at terminating focal atrial tachycardia than nodal or accessory pathway reentry, so persistence of an abnormal-P-wave rhythm after adenosine is the diagnostic clue. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with no neurologic symptoms has a 90% carotid stenosis on duplex. He has advanced cancer, and his expected survival is about two years. Which management is most appropriate?","choices":["Endarterectomy after one more duplex","Carotid endarterectomy","Medical management","Carotid angioplasty with stenting"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Surgery for an asymptomatic stenosis is advised only above 80% together with a life expectancy over five years. His limited survival means medical therapy: antiplatelets, risk-factor control and smoking cessation. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman who delivered a healthy baby three months ago now has breathlessness on exertion, orthopnea (breathlessness lying flat) and swollen ankles. She had no heart disease before pregnancy. Echocardiogram shows a dilated left ventricle with an ejection fraction of 30%. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Restrictive cardiomyopathy","Peripartum cardiomyopathy","Stress cardiomyopathy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Peripartum cardiomyopathy is a rare form of dilated cardiomyopathy that develops in the last trimester of pregnancy or within the first six months after delivery, as it has here. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"A 19-year-old college athlete has exertional chest pain without coronary disease and a harsh systolic murmur at the left sternal border. Hypertension and aortic valve disease have been excluded. Which echocardiogram finding confirms the clinical diagnosis of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Pericardial thickening on imaging","Unexplained wall of 1.5 cm or more","Dilated thin walls with low fraction","Apical ballooning and normal septum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The clinical diagnosis is confirmed when echocardiography shows unexplained left ventricular wall thickness of 1.5 cm or more anywhere in the wall, most often as asymmetric septal hypertrophy with a small cavity. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man becomes hypotensive and short of breath an hour after a catheter procedure perforates his heart. Echocardiogram shows only a small pericardial effusion, estimated at about 150 mL, with right ventricular diastolic collapse. Which best explains why so little fluid has compromised him?","choices":["Rapid filling of an unstretched sac","Chronic stretching of the sac","The pericardium was already calcified","The echocardiogram underestimated a liter"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acute effusions can produce tamponade with small amounts of fluid (under 200 mL) because the pericardium has no time to stretch, unlike chronic effusions that grow large over time. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man receiving chemotherapy for colon cancer comes to the emergency department with three days of left calf swelling that measures 4 cm more than the right, plus tenderness along the deep veins of the calf. He has no history of clots and nothing else explains his leg. The team debates whether to order a D-dimer or go straight to imaging. Using the Wells criteria, which is the best next step?","choices":["Venous duplex ultrasound","Ankle-brachial index","Contrast ascending venography","D-dimer testing first"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Active cancer, calf swelling over 3 cm and tenderness along the deep veins total 3 points, a high likelihood. A score of 3 or more skips the D-dimer and goes directly to venous duplex ultrasound of the leg. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man was treated for acute pericarditis 3 months ago and has been symptom-free for 6 weeks. His sharp, positional chest pain has now returned with a friction rub (a scratchy, squeaking sound). C-reactive protein is mildly raised. Which regimen is most appropriate?","choices":["Anakinra plus prednisone at once","Prednisone alone for 6 months","Ibuprofen with colchicine, stopped at 2 weeks","Ibuprofen with colchicine for 6 months or more"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. This is a first recurrence, symptoms returning after a symptom-free interval of 4 to 6 weeks or longer, treated with a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug plus colchicine for 6 months or more. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 37-year-old man with acute pericarditis feels better after 5 days of ibuprofen and colchicine and plans to stop both medicines now. Which counseling point is most important?","choices":["Switch to prednisone to finish faster","Stopping now is safe since the pain is gone","Finish the full course to prevent recurrence","Colchicine can stop; keep the ibuprofen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Patient education stresses adherence to the full treatment course to prevent recurrence, which affects about 15 to 30% of patients, together with activity restriction and red-flag symptoms. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 39-year-old woman with visible varicose veins asks what symptoms she is most likely to have. Which should she be told?","choices":["Sharp pain at rest when lying flat","Dull, achy heaviness after standing","Numb, burning feet at night","Calf cramping that eases with rest"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The most common symptom of varicose veins is a dull, achy heaviness or fatigue brought on by periods of standing, sometimes with itching over the veins. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with a known abdominal aortic aneurysm reports early satiety and pain isolated to his right groin, with no abdominal tenderness. Which explains the groin pain?","choices":["Renal colic from a stone","A femoral pseudoaneurysm","Pressure on the femoral nerve","An inguinal hernia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A symptomatic unruptured aneurysm can cause isolated groin pain from pressure on the femoral nerve, alongside abdominal, back or flank pain and early satiety as it expands. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A slow sinus rhythm that is causing hypotension and confusion is treated first with which drug?","choices":["Metoprolol","Digoxin","Atropine","Adenosine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Acute, symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable bradycardia is treated first with atropine, then a temporary pacemaker if it fails; chronic symptomatic disease needs a permanent pacemaker. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 2-month-old infant with a central line has poor feeding, fast breathing, tachycardia and a changing murmur. Blood cultures are positive and echocardiography shows a vegetation. Which complications are particularly common in infants because of septic emboli?","choices":["Janeway lesions and Roth spots","Glomerulonephritis","Heart failure from valve stenosis","Osteomyelitis, meningitis or pneumonia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Septic emboli are common in neonates and infants, producing osteomyelitis, meningitis or pneumonia, and neurologic signs such as seizures, hemiparesis and apnea. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman has viridans streptococcal endocarditis diagnosed early, before any heart failure or abscess, and she starts appropriate antibiotics the same day. Which statement about her outlook is most accurate?","choices":["Relatively good prognosis","Valve replacement in most","Highest mortality of any","Death within weeks is likely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Viridans streptococcal endocarditis has a relatively good prognosis if antibiotic treatment starts before complications occur. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man with atrial fibrillation at 170 has a blood pressure of 74/40 and is drowsy and confused. Which is the most appropriate immediate step?","choices":["Oral rate control","Anticoagulate for 21 days","Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous rate control"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hemodynamically unstable atrial fibrillation (hypotension and altered mental status) is treated with synchronized cardioversion. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man with complete heart block is stabilized with transcutaneous pacing in the emergency department. Which is the definitive treatment?","choices":["Implantable defibrillator","Catheter ablation","Permanent pacemaker","Oral beta blocker"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Transcutaneous pacing is the emergent bridge; the definitive treatment for complete (third-degree) atrioventricular block is a permanent pacemaker. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with chronic obstructive lung disease is admitted with an exacerbation. She is confused, and the monitor shows runs of skipped beats from frequent early, wide QRS complexes without P waves. Her arterial oxygen tension is low. Which underlying problem should be corrected first?","choices":["Her hypoxemia","Her blood glucose","Her blood pressure","Her body temperature"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hypoxemia is a main cause of premature ventricular contractions, along with electrolyte imbalance, so her low oxygen should be corrected first. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"In sick sinus syndrome, a fast run that is irregularly irregular with no P waves alternates with pauses. Which rhythm is the fast run?","choices":["Atrial fibrillation","Ventricular tachycardia","Sinus tachycardia","Multifocal atrial tachycardia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An irregularly irregular fast rhythm with no P waves is atrial fibrillation, and in tachy-brady syndrome, the form of sick sinus syndrome in which fast and slow rhythms alternate, atrial fibrillation is the most common tachyarrhythmia. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with diabetes and known black, shriveled toe tips now has a moist, swollen, blistered forefoot. His temperature is 39.2 C and heart rate 118. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Emergency surgical consultation","Start cilostazol and recheck","Observe until demarcation","Supervised exercise referral"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Wet gangrene (moist, edematous, blistering tissue with bacterial infection, often clostridial) is a surgical emergency because of the risk of sepsis, unlike the dry gangrene common in peripheral artery disease. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man has been on strict bed rest for six days during an admission for pneumonia and now has swelling and aching of the entire left leg. Which component of the Virchow triad does his prolonged bed rest represent?","choices":["Endothelial trauma","Arterial vasospasm","Hypercoagulable state","Venous stasis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bed rest is immobilization, which alters blood flow and lets blood pool in the leg veins, the venous stasis component of the Virchow triad. Prolonged immobilization raises the risk of deep vein thrombosis about twenty-fold. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Compared with Mobitz type I, Mobitz type II block carries a higher risk of which outcome?","choices":["Atrial fibrillation","Mobitz type I block","Sinus tachycardia","Complete heart block"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Mobitz type II has a higher risk of progression to complete (third-degree) heart block and sudden cardiac death, which is why it is treated even without symptoms. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman with a moderate pericardial effusion was stable yesterday. Today her blood pressure is 96/64, her jugular venous pressure is elevated, and she has new sinus tachycardia at 128. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Immediate pericardiocentesis","Repeat echocardiogram next week","Increase her loop diuretic","Rate control with a beta blocker"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In tamponade, sinus tachycardia may indicate significant hemodynamic compromise and is an indication for immediate pericardiocentesis, with fluid support until it is done. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has a painful ulcer above the medial malleolus with a clean base, fibrinous exudate and serous drainage, surrounded by brown pigmentation and pitting edema. Her pedal pulses are strong and her ankle-brachial index is 1.10. Which measure is essential for the ulcer to heal?","choices":["Topical antibiotic dressings alone","Early surgical valvuloplasty","Oral flavonoid therapy alone","Compression with edema control"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Venous ulcers will not heal unless edema is controlled and compression is applied, and an ankle-brachial index of 1.10 shows compression is safe. Wound care referral supports this. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman had a left leg deep vein thrombosis 11 months ago. She now has chronic left leg heaviness, edema and brownish ankle skin that worsen with standing and improve with elevation. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Post-thrombotic syndrome","Drug-induced edema","Lymphedema","Recurrent deep vein thrombosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Post-thrombotic (postphlebitic) syndrome is symptomatic chronic venous insufficiency that follows a deep vein thrombosis, appearing 6 to 24 months later, with heaviness, edema and brownish skin change from venous hypertension. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman with a femoral pseudoaneurysm after catheterization reports increasing thigh pain and tingling around the swelling. Which explains her pain?","choices":["Critical limb ischemia rest pain","Swelling and nerve compression","Retroperitoneal rupture of the aorta","Atheroemboli to the toes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A pseudoaneurysm presents with pain from increased pressure due to swelling or from nerve compression by the expanding blood-filled cavity. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 113"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has a story strongly suggesting constrictive pericarditis: edema, ascites, an elevated jugular venous pressure with the Kussmaul sign (a rise on inspiration). His chest radiograph shows no pericardial calcification. How should this radiograph be interpreted?","choices":["It rules out constrictive disease","It points to restrictive myopathy","It shows the cause is tamponade","It does not exclude constriction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pericardial calcification is highly consistent with constriction when present, but most patients do not have it on chest radiograph, so a film without calcium cannot rule the diagnosis out. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 26-year-old man has a sudden, regular narrow complex tachycardia at 230 with retrograde (inverted) P waves. An electrocardiogram taken last year in normal sinus rhythm showed a short PR interval and a delta wave (a slurred QRS upstroke). Which diagnosis is most likely?","choices":["Atrial fibrillation","Atrioventricular nodal reentry","Sinus tachycardia","Atrioventricular reentry"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pre-excitation (a short PR interval and a delta wave) on an earlier tracing in normal sinus rhythm points to an accessory pathway and the potential for atrioventricular reentrant tachycardia, which runs above 100, usually at 200 to 300, with hidden or retrograde P waves. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slides 14, 24, 33, 38"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman has 2 months of fever, weight loss and fatigue. A new murmur with a tumor plop (a low-pitched early diastolic sound) is heard, and three sets of blood cultures drawn before any antibiotics are negative. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Atrial myxoma","Lyme disease","Polymyalgia rheumatica","Subacute endocarditis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Atrial myxoma mimics endocarditis with fever and a new murmur, a tumor plop or mid-diastolic rumble, but it is a tumor and the cultures stay negative. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has a heart rate of 122 with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS. His temperature is 39.2°C and he has a productive cough and crackles at the right base. He is alert with a blood pressure of 126/78. Which is the best treatment for his heart rate?","choices":["Vagal maneuver","Synchronized cardioversion","Treat the infection and fever","Adenosine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A sinus tachycardia from fever and infection is an appropriate response to a pathologic process, and appropriate sinus tachycardia is managed by treating the underlying cause. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has had six days of worsening left leg pain and coolness. The limb has no sensory loss or weakness, and imaging shows an arterial thrombus. Which intervention fits this time frame?","choices":["Supervised exercise program","Major amputation","Oral aspirin alone","Catheter-directed thrombolysis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Intra-arterial catheter-directed thrombolysis, with alteplase as the thrombolytic, is indicated for acute limb ischemia of less than 14 days' duration. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which cardiac condition is listed among the risk factors for infective endocarditis?","choices":["Stable angina","Sinus bradycardia","Congenital heart disease","White coat hypertension"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Congenital heart disease is a listed risk factor, along with rheumatic heart disease, degenerative valve disease, intracardiac devices, prosthetic valves and injection drug use. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which definition fits cardiac tamponade?","choices":["Inflammation of the sac lasting under four weeks","Fibrous scarring that leaves the pericardium inelastic","Pericardial fluid under pressure compressing the heart","Extra pericardial fluid with no hemodynamic effect"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac tamponade is an accumulation of pericardial fluid under pressure, resulting in compression of the heart; it is one of the pericardial compressive syndromes. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with suspected endocarditis has a urinalysis sent as part of her workup. Which urinalysis result is most consistent with endocarditis?","choices":["Glucose and ketones","Bilirubin and urobilinogen","Blood, protein and white cells","Nitrites alone, no cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Urinalysis in endocarditis can show hematuria, proteinuria and pyuria, reflecting renal involvement such as glomerulonephritis or emboli. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man with painful blue toes and palpable pulses asks what the emboli in his toes are made of. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["Clot from the left atrium","Fibrin, platelets and cholesterol","Air from a catheter line","Infected valve vegetations"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Atheroembolism is multiple small deposits of fibrin, platelets and cholesterol debris embolizing from proximal atherosclerotic lesions to the small vessels of skin and muscle. — Arterial Occlusive Diseaase and Aneurysms - Carter.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman in stable, rate-controlled atrial flutter of unknown duration is to have elective outpatient cardioversion. Which must come first?","choices":["An adenosine challenge","A permanent pacemaker","21 days of anticoagulation","Catheter ablation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Outpatient cardioversion of atrial flutter is done after 21 days of anticoagulation, and anticoagulation is part of flutter management. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has an electrocardiogram before a sports tryout. The QRS is 0.14 seconds, with an rsR' pattern (a small upward wave, a dip, then a taller second upward wave) in V1 to V3 and a slurred S wave in leads I and aVL. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Accessory pathway","Ventricular tachycardia","Left bundle branch block","Right bundle branch block"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A QRS over 0.12 seconds with rsR' (bunny ears) in V1 to V3 and a slurred S wave in I and aVL is right bundle branch block. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man is readmitted 3 weeks after coronary artery bypass surgery with fever, pleuritic (worse with a deep breath) chest pain eased by sitting forward, and a friction rub (a scratchy, squeaking sound). His sternal wound looks clean. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Constrictive pericarditis","Dressler syndrome","Sternal wound infection","Acute graft occlusion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pericarditis weeks after myocardial infarction or post-cardiotomy (cardiac surgery) is Dressler syndrome, an inflammatory reaction to the injured tissue; aspirin plus colchicine treats it. — 31. Pericardial Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with restrictive cardiomyopathy has fatigue and exercise intolerance along with leg edema, hepatomegaly and ascites, but her lungs are relatively clear. Which pattern of heart failure is expected to dominate in this condition?","choices":["Isolated left-sided failure","Right-sided heart failure","Pure systolic failure","High-output heart failure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Although filling pressures are raised in both ventricles, right heart failure is often more prominent and may dominate in restrictive cardiomyopathy, giving edema, hepatomegaly and ascites. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman with a prosthetic valve and a past episode of anaphylaxis to penicillin needs a dental cleaning and can take pills. Which prophylactic agent should be avoided?","choices":["Azithromycin","Cephalexin","Doxycycline","Clarithromycin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cephalexin, cefazolin and ceftriaxone should not be used in patients with a history of anaphylaxis, angioedema or urticaria to penicillin or ampicillin. — 32. Infective Endocarditis FALL 2026 SV.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 74-year-old woman with newly diagnosed deep vein thrombosis has chronic kidney disease with a creatinine clearance below 30 mL/min. Which approach should be avoided?","choices":["Admission for intravenous heparin","Adjusting heparin by lab results","Overlapping heparin with warfarin","Beginning therapy as an outpatient"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Patients with a creatinine clearance below 30 mL/min are hospitalized to start treatment, because renal insufficiency also excludes outpatient therapy. Starting at home is the approach to avoid. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man in ventricular fibrillation arrest is shocked several times, and his rhythm changes to a flat line with no electrical activity in two leads. What does this change mean for his outlook?","choices":["No change in prognosis","A much worse prognosis","Successful defibrillation","A better chance of survival"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ventricular tachycardia and fibrillation have the best prognosis of the arrest rhythms; asystole has a poor prognosis and is usually an end-of-life rhythm. — Arrhythmias II - Elwaya  (1).pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What most commonly causes a lower extremity arteriovenous fistula?","choices":["Blunt trauma to the thigh","Groin access for a procedure","Surgical creation for dialysis","Congenital malformation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lower extremity fistulas are most commonly iatrogenic, from using the groin (femoral vessels) for percutaneous arterial and venous access in endovascular procedures. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"A 40-year-old woman has a regular narrow complex tachycardia at 182 with palpitations and mild anxiety. Her blood pressure is 128/80, oxygen saturation 98%, and she is alert without chest pain. Which treatment should be tried first?","choices":["Intravenous amiodarone","Vagal maneuver","Adenosine","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stable supraventricular tachycardia is treated first with a vagal maneuver, then adenosine (up to twice), keeping synchronized cardioversion for instability. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"}]};
